# Python
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*$py.class
*.egg-info/
dist/
# Anchored to the repo root. A bare build/ also matches docs/build/, which
# silently blocked committing the build workflow docs on 2026-07-26.
/build/
venv/
.venv/

# Environment. `.env` holds the graph password, the auth secret, the NCBI key
# and both OpenRouter keys, and has never been committed on any branch. The
# `.env.*` line covers variants that do not exist yet (.env.local, .env.production)
# so a future file cannot slip in under a name the single `.env` rule misses.
# `env.example` is deliberately NOT matched by either pattern: it is the tracked
# template and carries no values.
.env
.env.*

# Node/React
node_modules/
.next/
dist/
frontend/build/
frontend/test-results/
frontend/playwright-report/

# IDE
# .vscode/ contents stay local, with one exception: tasks.json can run commands
# automatically when a folder is opened, so a hostile edit or a newly planted
# file must surface as a git diff or an untracked file rather than silently.
# This is the ChainDrop/Shai-Hulud worm vector, the same one that targets
# .claude/settings.json. The pattern must be `.vscode/*`, not `.vscode/`: git
# cannot re-include a path whose parent directory is excluded, so a
# directory-level ignore would defeat the negation below.
.vscode/*
!.vscode/tasks.json
.idea/

# OS
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db

# Data and cache
*.sqlite
redis-data/

# Local agent OS: tracked again as of 2026-07-26, reversing the 2026-07-25
# untracking, for the duration of v1 development. Phase 5 rewrote most of the
# build harness inside .claude/, and while it was ignored none of that work
# could be reviewed in a pull request, reached CI, or survived a fresh clone.
# .codex is a git-tracked symlink to .claude (mode 120000), so it cannot drift.
# Revisit after v1 ships if the tooling should go back to being local-only.
#
# settings.local.json stays ignored: it is per-machine override state, holding
# absolute paths under a single user's home directory and an accumulated list
# of one-off command approvals that mean nothing on another machine.
.claude/settings.local.json

# Claude Security scan output (reports and working dirs, never committed)
CLAUDE-SECURITY-*/
security/

# Agent worktrees (transient, created by bossman-mode builders)
.claude/worktrees/

# Personal dev-workflow note (API keys/env-var setup for alternate model
# backends). Not repo doc content, stays local only.
docs/Claude_Code_model_fallback_setup.md

# Multi-model build-harness material (plan, cadence visualization). Names real
# providers, model identifiers and prices, which writing-style keeps out of
# tracked docs. Same reason as the note above, stays local only.
docs/build/multi-model-harness/

# macOS Finder/Archive Utility duplicate-copy artifacts (e.g. "env 2.py").
# These appear when a file is copied into a directory that already has a
# same-named file: Finder "Keep Both", or a zip extracted on top of an
# existing folder. Never a legitimate filename in this repo (verified
# 2026-07-28: no tracked file matches this shape). Root cause is moving files
# between a bossman-mode worktree and main via Finder/archive instead of git;
# this entry is the safety net, not the fix.
* [0-9].py
* [0-9].md
* [0-9].mako
* [0-9].json
* [0-9].ts
* [0-9].tsx
* [0-9].js
* [0-9].jsx
* [0-9][0-9].py
* [0-9][0-9].md

# Local session artifacts, product owner only, never committed
docs/artifacts/

# Coverage runs
.coverage

# TypeScript incremental build metadata, regenerated by `tsc -b`.
*.tsbuildinfo

# The Section 20.3 tool-call audit log. Append-only, never mutated after
# write, and it carries redacted tool params and returned record ids for
# every Layer 1, Layer 2 and Layer 3 access. Recorded as F-5.0-01: without
# this entry the default sink path is inside the working tree, so an
# ordinary `git add` would commit an operational audit trail into the
# repository's permanent history, where it cannot be removed by the
# append-only discipline that governs the file itself.
logs/

# JUnit XML reports the Section 24 gates write, at the repository root
# because that is the working directory a gate runs from. Three today, one
# per gate that produces a report: gate 4 (`unit-results.xml`), gate 5
# (`integration-results.xml`) and gate 9 (`required-paths.xml`). They are
# build artifacts, regenerated on every run and read by the gate that
# follows, never source.
#
# Added 2026-08-30 after two of them turned up untracked while closing
# build phase 5.0. Listed by name rather than matched by a glob, because
# `*-results.xml` would miss `required-paths.xml` anyway and a wider
# pattern would quietly swallow a file somebody meant to commit. The cost
# of the explicit list is that a NEW gate writing a report needs a line
# here; that is the intended trade, since adding one is a visible edit and
# a silently ignored file is not.
/unit-results.xml
/integration-results.xml
/required-paths.xml
